               with the use of Human Phenotype Ontology terms, undertook genome
               sequencing, applied automated variant prioritization on the
               basis of applied virtual gene panels and phenotypes, and
               identified novel pathogenic variants through research analysis.
               RESULTS: Diagnostic yields varied among family structures and
               were highest in family trios (both parents and a proband) and
               families with larger pedigrees. Diagnostic yields were much
               higher for disorders likely to have a monogenic cause (35\%)
               than for disorders likely to have a complex cause (11\%).
               Diagnostic yields for intellectual disability, hearing
               disorders, and vision disorders ranged from 40 to 55\%. We made
               genetic diagnoses in 25\% of the probands. A total of 14\% of
